      "updated": 1782074325310,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Q1 how would you scale this : Scaling ",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "containerId": "Pm4if_puyEvUiW0RbpKdN",
      "originalText": "Q1 how would you scale this : Scaling ",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "8jKBkQv3-dRLJhnrpFj4V",
      "type": "rectangle",
      "x": -878.643825373644,
      "y": 2077.2525596913692,
      "width": 986.0488047646621,
      "height": 1160,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffc9c9",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b23",
      "roundness": {
        "type": 3
      },
      "seed": 163632913,
      "version": 267,
      "versionNonce": 529446243,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "id": "zxQmctJhiwUTgKAJ6B950"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1782074403642,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false
    },
    {
      "id": "zxQmctJhiwUTgKAJ6B950",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -873.643825373644,
      "y": 2082.2525596913692,
      "width": 957.019287109375,
      "height": 1150,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffc9c9",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b24",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1832741791,
      "version": 610,
      "versionNonce": 997724419,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1782074403642,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Question 2:\nWhat if the library system scales to millions of books, members, and transactions?\n\nAnswer:\n\nI would separate the system into two layers:\n\n1. OLTP layer\n   This is used for real-time operations like borrow, return, availability check, and member updates.\n\n   Here I would use a normalized relational database.\n\n   Main tables:\n   - books\n   - book_copies\n   - members\n   - borrow_transactions\n   - returns\n\n2. OLAP layer\n   This is used for analytics and reporting.\n\n   Data from OLTP can be moved into a data lake or warehouse using batch or CDC ingestion.\n\n   Analytics storage:\n   - Parquet format\n   - Delta format\n   - Silver / Bronze / Gold layers\n   - Star schema for reporting\n\nImportant point:\n\nI would not use CSV for large-scale production analytics.\nCSV is easy to use, but it is slower and does not support schema enforcement, compression,\nindexing, or ACID transactions.\n\nBetter format:\n- Parquet for columnar storage\n- Delta Lake for ACID transactions and table versioning\n\nScaling techniques:\n- partition data by borrow_date or branch_id\n- use indexes on copy_id, member_id, book_id\n- use Spark for large-scale processing\n- create aggregated Gold tables for dashboards\n- separate OLTP workload from OLAP workload",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "containerId": "8jKBkQv3-dRLJhnrpFj4V",
      "originalText": "Question 2:\nWhat if the library system scales to millions of books, members, and transactions?\n\nAnswer:\n\nI would separate the system into two layers:\n\n1. OLTP layer\n   This is used for real-time operations like borrow, return, availability check, and member updates.\n\n   Here I would use a normalized relational database.\n\n   Main tables:\n   - books\n   - book_copies\n   - members\n   - borrow_transactions\n   - returns\n\n2. OLAP layer\n   This is used for analytics and reporting.\n\n   Data from OLTP can be moved into a data lake or warehouse using batch or CDC ingestion.\n\n   Analytics storage:\n   - Parquet format\n   - Delta format\n   - Silver / Bronze / Gold layers\n   - Star schema for reporting\n\nImportant point:\n\nI would not use CSV for large-scale production analytics.\nCSV is easy to use, but it is slower and does not support schema enforcement, compression, indexing, or ACID transactions.\n\nBetter format:\n- Parquet for columnar storage\n- Delta Lake for ACID transactions and table versioning\n\nScaling techniques:\n- partition data by borrow_date or branch_id\n- use indexes on copy_id, member_id, book_id\n- use Spark for large-scale processing\n- create aggregated Gold tables for dashboards\n- separate OLTP workload from OLAP workload",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "UvPYSfO2HtYtO5QiJYBc_",
      "type": "rectangle",
      "x": 751.9375149410755,
      "y": 2149.3046331658766,
      "width": 940.6998620927022,
      "height": 685,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffc9c9",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b25",
      "roundness": {
        "type": 3
      },
      "seed": 1513267281,
      "version": 1034,
      "versionNonce": 722758435,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "id": "oRrx0gyXNf0jnSJEuvEqs"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1782074430493,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false
    },
    {
      "id": "oRrx0gyXNf0jnSJEuvEqs",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 756.9375149410755,
      "y": 2154.3046331658766,
      "width": 917.37939453125,
      "height": 675,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffc9c9",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b26",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1907049439,
      "version": 1518,
      "versionNonce": 1078005443,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1782074430493,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Question 4:\nIf data is flowing in real time, how do we keep the system consistent?\n\nAnswer:\n\nFor real-time ingestion, I would keep the operational system as the source of truth.\n\nThe OLTP database should handle the actual borrow and return transactions.\n\nAfter the transaction is committed, the data can be sent to the data lake or analytics layer.\n\nFlow:\n\n1. User borrows book copy\n2. OLTP database validates availability\n3. Transaction is committed\n4. Change is captured using CDC or event streaming\n5. Data moves to Bronze layer\n6. Cleaned data moves to Silver layer\n7. Aggregated analytics move to Gold layer\n\nImportant point:\n\nThe data lake should not decide whether a book is available.\nThe OLTP database should decide availability.\n\nThe data lake is mainly for analytics, reporting, and historical analysis.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "containerId": "UvPYSfO2HtYtO5QiJYBc_",
      "originalText": "Question 4:\nIf data is flowing in real time, how do we keep the system consistent?\n\nAnswer:\n\nFor real-time ingestion, I would keep the operational system as the source of truth.\n\nThe OLTP database should handle the actual borrow and return transactions.\n\nAfter the transaction is committed, the data can be sent to the data lake or analytics layer.\n\nFlow:\n\n1. User borrows book copy\n2. OLTP database validates availability\n3. Transaction is committed\n4. Change is captured using CDC or event streaming\n5. Data moves to Bronze layer\n6. Cleaned data moves to Silver layer\n7. Aggregated analytics move to Gold layer\n\nImportant point:\n\nThe data lake should not decide whether a book is available.\nThe OLTP database should decide availability.\n\nThe data lake is mainly for analytics, reporting, and historical analysis.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
